\subsection{Research attempt}
\paragraph{Model, date and investigated scope.}
OpenAI GPT-6 Astra (configured model \texttt{gpt-6-astra}), ultra reasoning; generated 11 October 2026. Derives an admissible joint-scaling window from hypothetical quantitative errors and gives an explicit failure of inference from fixed-time convergence.

\paragraph{Sources and literature check.}
The complete supplied definition, including source qualifications and the dated literature assessment, was reviewed. The calculation below is an elementary restricted attempt motivated by [S2]; it does not claim novelty or a fresh literature-status determination.

\paragraph{Context and investigated scope.} The complete supplied definition, including source qualifications and the dated literature assessment, was reviewed. The calculation below is an elementary restricted attempt motivated by [S2]; it does not claim novelty or a fresh literature-status determination.

\paragraph{What a quantitative comparison would permit.} Suppose a normalized observable of the radius-$r$ gas differs from a kinetic surrogate by at most $Cr^\alpha t^p$ for $t\geq1$, while that surrogate differs from its long-time limit by at most $Ct^{-\beta}$, with $\alpha,p,\beta>0$. The triangle inequality then yields
\[
 |F_{r,t}-F_\infty|\leq C(r^\alpha t^p+t^{-\beta}).
\]
Choosing $t=r^{-\kappa}$ gives convergence whenever $0<\kappa<\alpha/p$. Balancing the two powers suggests $\kappa=\alpha/(p+\beta)$ and error order $r^{\alpha\beta/(p+\beta)}$. Every conclusion is conditional on the two explicit uniform estimates.

\paragraph{Why iterated convergence is too weak.} The bounded scalar function $G(r,t)=rt/(1+rt)$ tends to zero as $r\to0$ for each fixed $t$, but along $t=1/r$ it equals one half and along $t=r^{-2}$ it tends to one. Thus fixed-time Boltzmann--Grad convergence contains no automatic long-time window. This elementary obstruction persists even if a separate surrogate has a well-defined long-time limit.

\paragraph{Source-level gap.} No rates $\alpha,p,\beta$ are established here for the periodic Lorentz gas, whose recollisions and long flights can alter the appropriate transport normalization. The source does not demand convergence along all arbitrary $(r,t)$ paths, and that stronger claim is not adopted. The first gap is a uniform-in-time collision-history comparison on an explicit growing window, combined with the correctly normalized long-time theorem. The conditional balancing calculation specifies what such estimates would need to accomplish.

\paragraph{Verification and adversarial audit.}
